<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1874 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The anthem of Hawaii, with words written by King Kalākaua in 1874 when Hawaii was an independent kingdom.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>David Kalākaua</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Henri Berger</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Native Hawaiians; the Hawaiian Kingdom and the people of Hawaii</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Hawaiian Kingdom (present-day Hawaii, United States)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1874; adopted as national anthem 1876</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by King David Kalākaua (1874; he died in 1891))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii-ponoi">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</a>&quot; (&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>'s Own&quot;) was written in 1874 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> of the Hawaiian Kingdom. The music is by Captain Henri Berger, then the king's royal bandmaster. Its melody recalls &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; and the Prussian anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a>&quot;.</p><p>In 1876 it was adopted as the national anthem of the Hawaiian Kingdom, replacing &quot;He Mele Lāhui Hawaiʻi&quot;, a composition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a>. It remained the national anthem under the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexed Hawaii in 1898 it continued in use as the islands' own song, the adopted song of the Territory of Hawaii.</p><p>In 1967 an act of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> State Legislature made it the official anthem of the U.S. state of Hawaii. It is commonly sung at sporting events in Hawaii immediately after the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and at many schools and at the meetings and events of local service organisations.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A national anthem of loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and its own people, written by a reigning king. Its tone is stately, in the manner of the European royal anthems its melody recalls.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hawaiian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hawaiian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Hawaiʻi ponoʻī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hawaiʻi's very own</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nānā i kou mōʻī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look to your king</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ka lani aliʻi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The royal chief</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Ke aliʻi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The chief.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Makua lani ē,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O royal father,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Kamehameha ē,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Kamehameha,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Na kāua e pale</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We two shall defend</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Me ka ihe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With the spear.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Hawaiʻi ponoʻī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hawaiʻi's very own</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nānā i nā aliʻi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look to the chiefs</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nā pua muli kou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The offspring after you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Nā pōkiʻi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The younger ones.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">Hawaiʻi ponoʻī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hawaiʻi's very own</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E ka lāhui ē</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O people (nation)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">ʻO kāu hana nui</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your great work</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="haw">E ui ē.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Strive [?] for it.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: King David Kalākaua (1874); the Hawaiian text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hawai%CA%BBi_Pono%CA%BB%C4%AB" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1 (and 3.1, 4.1): 'Hawaiʻi ponoʻī' is 'Hawaiʻi's own', i.e. the true, native people of Hawaiʻi, whom the song addresses.</li><li>line 1.3: 'Ka lani aliʻi' can be 'the heavenly (royal) chief'; 'lani' is sky, heaven, and a word for royalty.</li><li>line 2.1: 'Makua lani' is 'royal (heavenly) father', meaning Kamehameha I, not God.</li><li>line 2.3: 'kāua' is the dual inclusive 'you and I', so 'we two (you and I) shall defend'.</li><li>line 3.3: 'Nā pua muli kou' is literally 'the flowers after you': the descendants, the generations that follow.</li><li>line 3.4: 'pōkiʻi' is younger sibling(s); here the younger generation, the people as younger kin of the chiefs.</li><li>line 4.4: 'E ui ē': 'ui' in the dictionaries is 'to ask, to question'; the line is usually understood as an urging ('strive, press on'), but the sense is not certain. 'Uʻi' (youthful, vigorous) is another possible reading of the unmarked word.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
